WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.044
Before a computer can improve how a chip is split, it needs a first split.

00:00:05.044 --> 00:00:08.520
That first split is called the initial bipartition.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.814
You will meet three ways to build one: random, greedy and grow.

00:00:12.814 --> 00:00:17.858
You will see the same small graph get a cut of three, or a cut of sixteen.

00:00:17.858 --> 00:00:23.515
And you will learn the simple habit that fixes it: try several starts, and keep the

00:00:23.515 --> 00:00:23.856
best.

00:00:23.856 --> 00:00:29.205
A chip is made of small building blocks called cells, joined by wires.

00:00:29.205 --> 00:00:32.414
We draw each cell as a dot, called a node.

00:00:32.414 --> 00:00:38.451
A line between two nodes is an edge: it means the two cells are wired together.

00:00:38.451 --> 00:00:43.494
The number on an edge is its weight: how many wires join the pair.

00:00:43.494 --> 00:00:46.169
Our example has five cells, A to E.

00:00:46.169 --> 00:00:50.448
The heaviest edges, A, B and D, E, have five wires each.

00:00:50.448 --> 00:00:54.286
Splitting a chip into two sides is called a bipartition.

00:00:54.286 --> 00:00:59.357
Edges that cross between the two sides are cut, and they are drawn in red.

00:00:59.357 --> 00:01:03.880
The cutsize adds up their weights: here, two plus one makes three.

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:09.225
Putting every cell on one side gives a cut of zero, but that split is useless.

00:01:09.225 --> 00:01:13.131
So we also check balance: how many cells each side holds.

00:01:13.131 --> 00:01:17.928
A split is legal when no side holds more than three of the five cells.

00:01:17.928 --> 00:01:22.328
Labs four and five teach two famous methods, called KL and FM.

00:01:22.328 --> 00:01:26.870
They improve a split by moving cells from one side to the other.

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:31.483
But they can only improve: they need a legal split to start from.

00:01:31.483 --> 00:01:34.180
Think of picking two teams for a game.

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:39.431
First you need any two teams, then you swap players to make the game fair.

00:01:39.431 --> 00:01:42.696
A good first split leaves less swapping to do.

00:01:42.696 --> 00:01:45.720
Here is our graph again, before any split.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:50.616
It has five cells, so the sides will hold two cells and three cells.

00:01:50.616 --> 00:01:55.080
Every method reports two numbers: the cutsize and the balance.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:58.680
Let's build a first split in three different ways.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:00.322
Method one is random.

00:02:00.322 --> 00:02:06.266
Write the five names on cards, shuffle them, and deal two cards to one side.

00:02:06.266 --> 00:02:09.629
The other three cards go to the other side.

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:13.617
A computer shuffles using a number called the seed.

00:02:13.617 --> 00:02:17.058
The same seed always gives the same shuffle.

00:02:17.058 --> 00:02:20.030
With seed five, D and E land together.

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:24.096
Only C, D and C, E are cut, so the cutsize is three.

00:02:24.096 --> 00:02:28.632
That is the best split this graph has, found by pure luck.

00:02:28.632 --> 00:02:33.805
Method two is greedy: at each step, it takes what looks best right now.

00:02:33.805 --> 00:02:38.467
It starts by putting both ends of the heaviest edge on one side.

00:02:38.467 --> 00:02:44.004
A, B and D, E both weigh five, so a fixed rule picks the smaller name: A, B.

00:02:44.004 --> 00:02:49.322
D starts the other side: it has no wire to A or B, and it comes before E.

00:02:49.322 --> 00:02:53.402
C joins A and B, with five wires there against two to D.

00:02:53.402 --> 00:02:57.846
Last, E joins D, which cuts only the edge of weight one to C.

00:02:57.846 --> 00:02:59.376
The cutsize is three.

00:02:59.376 --> 00:03:00.913
Method three is grow.

00:03:00.913 --> 00:03:07.062
Pick a start node, and grow a side around it, like a drop of ink spreading on paper.

00:03:07.062 --> 00:03:12.406
At each step, add the node behind the heaviest edge that leaves the side.

00:03:12.406 --> 00:03:13.357
Start from D.

00:03:13.357 --> 00:03:17.383
The heaviest edge out of D goes to E, with weight five.

00:03:17.383 --> 00:03:22.580
Now the side holds two of the five cells, which is enough, so it stops.

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:27.192
A, B and C form the other side, and the cutsize is three again.

00:03:27.192 --> 00:03:30.518
So far, all three methods found the same split.

00:03:30.518 --> 00:03:32.287
But that was partly luck.

00:03:32.287 --> 00:03:37.170
Other seeds and other start nodes give cuts from three up to sixteen.

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:41.134
Each of those splits is legal, so none of them is wrong.

00:03:41.134 --> 00:03:46.512
That is why we build several starts, and keep the one with the smallest cut.

00:03:46.512 --> 00:03:49.391
Now let's see how bad luck can get.

00:03:49.391 --> 00:03:53.914
With seed three, the shuffle deals B and D to one side.

00:03:53.914 --> 00:03:56.875
A, C and E end up on the other side.

00:03:56.875 --> 00:03:59.754
Let's add up the cut, edge by edge.

00:03:59.754 --> 00:04:04.936
A, B five, B, C four, C, D two and D, E five: sixteen in total.

00:04:04.936 --> 00:04:10.364
Both heavy edges are cut, so this is the worst legal split of all.

00:04:10.364 --> 00:04:16.368
Out of the sixteen seeds we tried, only three give the best cut of three.

00:04:16.368 --> 00:04:21.250
Grow has no luck in it, but it has a choice: the start node.

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:23.365
Start from A instead of D.

00:04:23.365 --> 00:04:28.979
The heaviest edge out of A goes to B, with weight five, so B joins A.

00:04:28.979 --> 00:04:32.803
With two cells, the side is full, and it stops.

00:04:32.803 --> 00:04:37.766
Now C, D and E form the other side, so A, C and B, C are cut.

00:04:37.766 --> 00:04:41.997
One plus four makes five, which is worse than three.

00:04:41.997 --> 00:04:45.984
Starting from C is even worse: a cutsize of nine.

00:04:45.984 --> 00:04:49.691
The fix is simple: never trust a single start.

00:04:49.691 --> 00:04:55.171
Build several: a few random seeds, greedy, and grow from every node.

00:04:55.171 --> 00:04:58.959
Throw away any split that breaks the size rule.

00:04:58.959 --> 00:05:01.779
Keep the one with the smallest cut.

00:05:01.779 --> 00:05:08.145
On our graph, greedy and the five grows give three, five, five, nine, three and

00:05:08.145 --> 00:05:08.629
three.

00:05:08.629 --> 00:05:12.336
The best is three: A, B and C against D and E.

00:05:12.336 --> 00:05:17.138
Here is a star: A sits in the middle, wired to every other cell.

00:05:17.138 --> 00:05:21.190
A, B weighs nine, and the four other edges weigh five.

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:24.942
With six cells, each side must hold exactly three.

00:05:24.942 --> 00:05:30.120
An older greedy had no size limit, so it kept adding cells next to A.

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:35.072
It ended with five cells against one: cutsize five, but not legal.

00:05:35.072 --> 00:05:39.575
Our greedy stops each side at three cells, and gets fifteen.

00:05:39.575 --> 00:05:42.576
No legal split of this star does better.

00:05:42.576 --> 00:05:46.428
In this graph, A, B and D, E both weigh five again.

00:05:46.428 --> 00:05:49.676
Greedy must pick one of them to start from.

00:05:49.676 --> 00:05:55.114
The rule picks the smaller name, A, B, and that gives a cutsize of nine.

00:05:55.114 --> 00:05:58.966
Starting from D, E instead gives a cutsize of four.

00:05:58.966 --> 00:06:02.516
So a simple tie rule more than doubled the cut.

00:06:02.516 --> 00:06:08.256
Grow from C also finds four, which is one more reason to try several starts.

00:06:08.256 --> 00:06:13.182
Here are two triangles, joined by one bridge, C, D, with weight four.

00:06:13.182 --> 00:06:15.537
Every triangle edge weighs three.

00:06:15.537 --> 00:06:20.891
The natural split puts one triangle on each side, and cuts only the bridge.

00:06:20.891 --> 00:06:26.388
But greedy starts from the heaviest edge, and here that is the bridge itself.

00:06:26.388 --> 00:06:31.028
So C and D begin on the same side, and the cut ends up at twelve.

00:06:31.028 --> 00:06:36.168
Grow from A keeps its triangle together, and cuts only the bridge: four.

00:06:36.168 --> 00:06:39.763
In the browser lab, you work on the same small graph.

00:06:39.763 --> 00:06:45.460
You can move cells from one side to the other, and watch the cutsize and the balance

00:06:45.460 --> 00:06:45.935
change.

00:06:45.935 --> 00:06:50.343
You can also run random, greedy and grow, and compare their cuts.

00:06:50.343 --> 00:06:55.023
The challenges check the split you built, not the button you clicked.

00:06:55.023 --> 00:06:59.160
For example, find the start node that gives the smallest cut.

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:03.176
If you like programming, write the three methods in Python.

00:07:03.176 --> 00:07:07.873
The examples file gives the exact rules, including how to break ties.

00:07:07.873 --> 00:07:11.481
First, run the checker to print every correct answer.

00:07:11.481 --> 00:07:15.838
Then write your own functions, and run the checker on your file.

00:07:15.838 --> 00:07:21.079
It also feeds the graph in reverse order, so a missing tie rule shows up as a

00:07:21.079 --> 00:07:21.624
failure.

00:07:21.624 --> 00:07:24.016
Watch out for four common mistakes.

00:07:24.016 --> 00:07:26.886
Reporting the cutsize without the balance.

00:07:26.886 --> 00:07:32.626
Letting one side grow past its limit, which makes the cut look better than it really

00:07:32.626 --> 00:07:32.831
is.

00:07:32.831 --> 00:07:38.434
Trusting a single random seed: on our graph, the cut ranged from three to sixteen.

00:07:38.434 --> 00:07:43.901
And breaking ties by the order of the input, so the answer changes when the file

00:07:43.901 --> 00:07:44.448
changes.

00:07:44.448 --> 00:07:47.923
Methods like KL and FM need a legal first split.

00:07:47.923 --> 00:07:52.411
Random shuffles and deals: it is fast, but it depends on luck.

00:07:52.411 --> 00:07:58.058
Greedy keeps the heaviest edge inside, then puts each cell where it cuts less.

00:07:58.058 --> 00:08:02.257
Grow spreads from a start node, so the start node matters.

00:08:02.257 --> 00:08:05.370
Ties and size limits can change the answer.

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:10.944
So build several starts, keep only the legal ones, and pick the smallest cut.

00:08:10.944 --> 00:08:12.370
Now it's your turn.

